Kubernetes problem-solving questions ans answers 
✅ 1. Pods are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff. How do you troubleshoot?

Answer:

Check the logs of the crashing pod:

kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

kubectl logs <pod-name> --previous

kubectl get events -n <name Space> |grep -i <pod Name> (Very Good command)

Note: Please keep this in mind — if a pod is in a NotReady state, always use:

kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

This is because kubectl logs may not return any output when the pod is not ready, especially if:

The container has not started yet.

The pod is still initializing (e.g., stuck in Init containers),

Or the container crashed before any logs were generated.

Common causes:

Misconfigured startup command

Application crash due to missing config or DB

Readiness or liveness probe failures

Fix by updating the deployment or correcting the environment/configMap/secrets.

✅ 2. You get an ImagePullBackOff error. How do you resolve it?

Answer:

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> (to see image-related errors).

Common causes:

Invalid image name or tag

Missing DockerHub credentials for private repos

Network/DNS issues

Fix:

Correct image in the manifest

Add imagePullSecrets if a private repo is used

Check node DNS resolution and internet access

✅ 3. A service is not accessible via ClusterIP. How to debug?

Answer:

Check service and pod IPs:

kubectl get svc, pods -o wide (Use nslookup or dig from within the cluster:)

kubectl run -it --rm test --image=busybox -- /bin/sh

nslookup <service-name>

Use curl or wget to test endpoints

Check network policies (if any) or service selector mismatch.

✅ 4. Node is in NotReady state. What steps would you take?

Answer:

Check node status:

kubectl describe node <node-name> (SSH into the node and check kubelet:)

systemctl status kubelet

Verify disk pressure, memory pressure, or network issues.

Restart kubelet and container runtime:

systemctl restart kubelet docker # or containerd

✅ 5. Deployments are not scaling as expected (e.g., HPA is not triggering). Why?

Answer:

Check if metrics-server is deployed and running:

kubectl get deployment metrics-server -n kube-system

kubectl top pods (to confirm metrics availability.)

Describe the HPA:

kubectl describe hpa <deployment-name>

Fix:

Ensure correct resource requests/limits are set

Ensure HPA target metrics (CPU/memory) are configured properly

Note: Another very useful command for troubleshooting is journalctl, which should be run directly on the Kubernetes node where the pod is scheduled.

You can use the following command to check recent system logs related to a specific pod:

journalctl --since "6 hours ago" --until "now" | grep -i <pod-name> (Depending on the use case, you just change the hour. Like here, I mentioned 6, you just change it to 2 or 3)

This helps identify:

Kubelet-level issues,

Container runtime errors (e.g., Docker, containerd),

Or any low-level system events affecting the pod
